#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *
#import "symbols.typ": *

#heading(
  level: 2,
)[The integral definition and basic properties of a local residue]
<sec:local-residue>

#subsection[The integral definition] <ss:integral-definition>
The local residue that we consider here is the integral of a meromorphic form of
a very general kind and is associated with a holomorphic mapping
$f = (f_1, ..., f_n): overline(scr(U))_a -> CC^n$ defined in the closure of some
bounded neighborhood $scr(U)_a$ of a point $a in CC^n$. We assume that $f$ has
an isolated zero at the point $a: overline(scr(U))_a inter f^(-1) (0) = {a}$.
#index-mark(
  "Residue",
  sub: "local with respect to a mapping",
)The local residue of a meromorphic form
$omega = h d z \/ f_1 dots.c f_n$#notation-mark($h d z \/ f$, sort: "h d z f")
($d z = d z_1 and dots.c and d z_n$) at the point $a$ is the integral
$
  res_a omega = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a) omega
  = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a) (h d z)/f
$ <eq:local-residue>
over the real $n$-dimensional chain
$
  Gamma_a = Gamma_a^epsilon (f)
  = {z in scr(U)_a: |f_j (z)| = epsilon_j, quad j = 1, ..., n},
$
#notation-mark(
  $Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$,
  sort: "Gamma epsilon f a",
)where $epsilon_j in RR_>$ are sufficiently small positive real numbers such
that $Gamma_a^epsilon (f) relcomp scr(U)_a$. The orientation of the chain
$Gamma_a$ is determined by the condition
$d(arg f_1) and dots.c and d(arg f_n) >= 0$.

Let us give some explanations. In our conditions the mapping $f$ does not have
zeros on the compact set $partial scr(U)_a$, the boundary of $scr(U)_a$.
Therefore there is a number $delta in RR_>$ such that the inverse image
$f^(-1) (scr(B)_delta)$ of a closed polydisk of radius $delta$ is compact in
$scr(U)_a$. But because $f^(-1) (scr(B)_delta)$ is characterized by #source(27)
the condition $|f_j| <= delta$, for all $epsilon_j < delta$ the chains
$Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ are relatively compact in $scr(U)_a$, and so they are
cycles homologous to each other in the domain
$scr(U)_a without {z: f_1 (z) dots.c f_n (z) = 0}$. The form $omega$ is closed
in this domain (as a holomorphic form of highest degree), and by Stokes's
formula (@ss:stokes) the integrals @eq:local-residue are equal for all cycles
$Gamma_a = Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ if $epsilon_j <= delta$. We have thus explained
why a local residue is well defined. Moreover, from Sard's theorem, applied to
the mapping $(|f_1|^2, ..., |f_n|^2): scr(U)_a -> RR_+^n$, it follows that for
almost all $epsilon in [0, delta]^n subset RR_+^n$ the cycles
$Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ are smooth manifolds. Therefore in the definition
@eq:local-residue we can assume that $Gamma_a$ is a smooth cycle whose
orientation is determined by the order of the parameters
$theta_1 = arg f_1, ..., theta_n = arg f_n$ that take part in its parametric
representation $f_j (z) = epsilon_j e^(i theta_j)$, $j = 1, ..., n$ (see
@ss:orientation). The cycle $Gamma_a^epsilon (f)$ will be called a #index-mark(
  "Cycles",
  sub: "local",
)_local cycle_ for the reason that its support "tends" to the point $a$ as
$epsilon -> 0$. We shall also call the integral @eq:local-residue the _local
residue of the function $h$_ (the form $h d z$) _with respect to the holomorphic
mapping $f$_ at the point $a$ and denote it by $res_a ""_f (h)$#notation-mark(
  $res_a ""_f (h)$,
  sort: "res a f",
) ($res_a ""_f (h d z)$). The local residue @eq:local-residue coincides with the
Grothendieck residue symbol
$
  Res mat(delim: "[", h d z; f_1\, ...\, f_n)
$
that is well known in algebraic geometry (see @bib:Beauville1971,
@bib:Hartshorne1966).

Let us mention some of the simplest properties of the local residue. First, from
the definition of the orientation of the cycle $Gamma_a$ it is obvious that the
local residue is skew-symmetric with respect to permutations of the components
$f_j$ of the mapping $f$:
$
  res_((f_1, ..., f_n)) = (sgn tau) res_((f_(tau(1)), ..., f_(tau(n)))).
$
#new-par
In order to formulate the next property, we observe that by taking account of
the explanations given for the definition @eq:local-residue, the local residue
$res_a ""_f (h)$ is actually defined for elements $h$ of the ring of germs
$scr(O)_a$ of functions holomorphic at the point $a$. We denote by $I_a (f)$ the
ideal in $scr(O)_a$ generated by the components $f_1, ..., f_n$ of the mapping
$f$.

#proposition[
  If $h in I_a (f)$, then $res_a ""_f (h) = 0$.
] <prop:ideal-residue-zero>

#proof[
  The condition $h in I_a (f)$ implies that $h = q_1 f_1 + dots.c + q_n f_n$,
  $q_j in scr(O)_a$. In this case
  $
    res_a ""_f (h) = sum_(k = 1)^n (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a)
    (q_k d z)/(f_1 dots.c [k] dots.c f_n).
  $ <eq:ideal-residue-sum>
  We observe that on the right we have the sum of integrals of closed forms over
  cycles homologous to zero in the domains of regularity of these forms. In
  fact, $Gamma_a = (-1)^(k - 1) partial c_k$, $k = 1, ..., n$, where
  $c_k = {z in scr(U)_a: |f_j (z)| = epsilon_j, j != k; |f_k (z)| <= epsilon_k}$
  is a compact $(n + 1)$-chain in
  $scr(U)_a without {z: f_1 (z) dots.c [k] dots.c f_n (z) = 0}$ #source(28)
  ($epsilon_j$ is assumed to be so small that
  $f^(-1) (scr(B)_epsilon) relcomp scr(U)_a$) whose orientation is determined by
  the order of the parameters
  $theta_1, ..., theta_(k - 1), r_k, theta_k, theta_(k + 1), ..., theta_n$ in
  its parametric representation $f_j (z) = epsilon_j e^(i theta_j)$, $j != k$;
  $f_k (z) = r_k e^(i theta_k)$; $theta_j in [0, 2 pi]$,
  $r_k in [0, epsilon_k]$. Thus by Stokes' theorem each term in
  @eq:ideal-residue-sum is equal to zero.
]

#subsection[The geometric multiplicity of a mapping]
<ss:geometric-multiplicity>
Let $f: scr(U)_a -> CC^n$ be a holomorphic mapping of the neighborhood
$scr(U)_a subset CC^n$, where $f(a) = 0$. The point $a$ is called a #index-mark(
  "Zero, simple",
)_simple zero_ of the mapping $f$ if the Jacobian $J_f = partial f \/ partial z$
is nonzero at the point $a$. From the implicit function theorem it follows that
a simple zero is always isolated. If $J_f (a) = 0$, then $a$ is called a
_multiple zero_ of the mapping $f$. Geometric multiplicity shows how many simple
zeros a multiple zero splits into under small "perturbations" of the mapping
$f$.

#proposition[
  Let $a$ be an isolated zero of a holomorphic mapping
  $f(z): overline(scr(U))_a -> CC^n$, where the closure $overline(scr(U))_a$
  does not contain zeros of $f$ other than $z = a$. Then there is a positive
  number $epsilon$ such that for almost all $zeta$ from the polydisk
  $scr(B)_epsilon = {|zeta_j| < epsilon}$ the mapping $w = f(z) - zeta$ has only
  simple zeros in $scr(U)_a$, where the number of them is finite and does not
  depend on the choice of such $zeta$ and the neighborhood $scr(U)_a$. The
  number of such simple zeros is called the (geometric) #index-mark(
    "Multiplicity",
    sub: "geometric",
  )multiplicity of $f$ at $a$ and is denoted by $mu_a (f)$#notation-mark(
    $mu_a (f)$,
    sort: "mu a f",
  ).
] <prop:multiplicity>

#remark[
  We shall also use the terms "multiplicity of the zero $a$ of the mapping $f$"
  or "multiplicity of the root $a$ of the system of equations $f = 0$." If $a$
  is a nonisolated zero of $f$, we put $mu_a (f) = infinity$.
] <rem:multiplicity-terms>

#proof(title: [Proof of Proposition~@prop:multiplicity.])[
  We use the Martinelli-Bochner integral representation
  (@bib:Shabat1976[p.~87]): if $G subset CC^n$ is a domain with piecewise smooth
  boundary, then for any function $phi$ holomorphic in $G$ and continuous in the
  closure $overline(G)$ we have
  $
    phi(z) = integral_(partial G) phi(zeta) beta(zeta - z),
  $ <eq:martinelli-bochner>
  where the kernel $beta$ (the #index-mark(
    "Kernel, Martinelli-Bochner",
  )_Martinelli-Bochner kernel_)#notation-mark($beta(f)$, sort: "beta f") is
  equal to#ed-note[Here $CC^n$ is oriented by
    $d x_1 and dots.c and d x_n and d y_1 and dots.c and d y_n$, as in Aizenberg
    and Yuzhakov @bib:Aizenberg1983c, Preliminaries, 1°. With the orientation
    $d x_1 and d y_1 and dots.c and d x_n and d y_n$, given by the parameters
    $r_1, theta_1, ..., r_n, theta_n$ of @ss:total-sum-theorem and used in
    @sec:representability, the kernel @eq:martinelli-bochner-kernel and the form
    @eq:eta-form of @ss:boundary-representation take the factor
    $(-1)^(n (n - 1) \/ 2)$.]
  $
    beta(u) = frac((n - 1)!, (2 pi i)^n |u|^(2n)) sum_(k = 1)^n
    (-1)^(k - 1) overline(u)_k d overline(u) [k] and d u,
  $ <eq:martinelli-bochner-kernel>
  $
    |u|^2 = |u_1|^2 + dots.c + |u_n|^2, wide
    d overline(u) [k] = d overline(u)_1 and dots.c [k] dots.c d overline(u)_n.
  $
  We observe that the form $beta(u)$ is closed and its set of singularities
  consists only of the point $u = 0$. Here we require only the special case of
  @eq:martinelli-bochner (when $phi equiv 1$, $z = 0$, and
  $partial G = S^(2n - 1)$, a sphere with center at the origin of the space
  $CC_zeta^n$): $integral_(S^(2n - 1)) beta(zeta) = 1$. Substituting in this
  formula instead of $zeta$ the biholomorphic mapping $zeta = Phi(z)$, we obtain
  $
    integral_(S^(2n - 1)) beta(Phi) = 1.
  $ <eq:sphere-integral>
]

#lemma[
  #source(29)If $f: G -> CC^n$ is a holomorphic mapping of the domain
  $G subset CC^n$, then for any cycle
  $gamma in Z_(2n - 1) (G without f^(-1) (0))$ the integral
  $integral_gamma beta(f)$ is an integer.
] <lem:integer-integral>

#proof[
  We observe that $f$ maps $G without f^(-1) (0)$ into
  $CC_*^n = CC^n without {0}$. Since the homology class of the sphere
  $S^(2n - 1)$ generates the whole homology group $H_(2n - 1) (CC_*^n)$ (see
  @ss:mayer-vietoris), we have $f_\# (gamma) ~ N dot S^(2n - 1)$ for some
  integer $N$. Moreover, the form $beta(f)$ is obtained from $beta(zeta)$ by a
  change of variables $zeta = f(z)$ (this fact is written thus:
  $beta(f) = f^\# (beta)$). It now remains to use the formula for change of
  variables ($integral_(f_\# (gamma)) omega = integral_gamma f^\# (omega)$, see
  @ss:differential-forms) and formula @eq:sphere-integral:
  $
    integral_gamma beta(f) = integral_gamma f^\# (beta)
    = integral_(f_\# (gamma)) beta(zeta)
    = integral_(N dot S^(2n - 1)) beta(zeta) = N.
  $
]

Let us apply the assertion of the lemma to the cycle $gamma = partial scr(U)_a$
and the mapping $w = f(z) - zeta$, where
$|zeta_j| < epsilon := min_(partial scr(U)_a) max_k |f_k|$. In this case the
integral $N(zeta) = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) beta(f - zeta)$ takes integer
values. But since for any point $z in partial scr(U)_a$ and at least one
$j in {1, ..., n}$ we have
$|f_j (z) - zeta_j| >= |f_j (z)| - |zeta_j| >= epsilon - |zeta_j| > 0$, this
integral is continuous with respect to the parameter $zeta in scr(B)_epsilon$
(we recall that the form $beta(f - zeta)$ is singular only when $f - zeta = 0$)
and so it is equal to an integer $N$ that does not depend on $zeta$. By Sard's
theorem, for almost all $zeta in scr(B)_epsilon$ the zeros of the mapping
$w = f - zeta$ are simple and therefore isolated. In addition, from the
condition $|f(z) - zeta| > 0$, $z in partial scr(U)_a$, it follows that the
number of such zeros is finite; we denote them by $z^((nu)) (zeta)$,
$nu = 1, ..., mu(zeta)$. For each such zero
$integral_(S_nu) beta(f - zeta) = 1$, where $S_nu$ is a sphere with center in
$z^((nu)) (zeta)$ inside which there are no other zeros $z^((k)) (zeta)$,
$k != nu$. Now, taking into account that
$partial scr(U)_a ~ sum_(nu = 1)^(mu(zeta)) S_nu$ in the domain
$scr(U)_a without {f - zeta = 0}$ (that is, the domain of regularity of the form
$beta(f - zeta)$), we obtain
$
  mu(zeta) = sum_(nu = 1)^(mu(zeta)) integral_(S_nu) beta(f - zeta)
  = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) beta(f - zeta)
  = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) beta(f) = N.
$ <eq:number-of-zeros>
Thus, the number $mu(zeta)$ of simple zeros of the mapping $w = f - zeta$ is the
same for almost all $zeta in scr(B)_epsilon$.

From formula @eq:number-of-zeros we have actually proved the following result.

#proposition[
  If a mapping $f: overline(scr(U)) -> CC^n$ does not have zeros on the boundary
  $partial scr(U)$, then the integral $integral_(partial scr(U)) beta(f)$ is
  equal to the number of zeros (taking account of multiplicity) of the mapping
  $f$ inside $scr(U)$.
] <prop:zeros-count>

Hence, since the integral $integral_(partial scr(U)) beta(f)$ is integer-valued,
we obtain the following result.

#proposition(title: [#index-mark(
    "Principle",
    sub: "of Rouché",
    target: <prop:rouche>,
  )Rouché's principle])[
  If holomorphic mappings $f_0, f_1: overline(scr(U)) -> CC^n$ are homotopic on
  the boundary $partial scr(U)$ (that is, there is a homotopy
  $F(t, z): [0, 1] times overline(scr(U)) -> CC^n$ between $f_0$ and $f_1$ that
  is holomorphic with respect #source(30) to $z$, continuous with respect to
  $t$, and not equal to zero on $partial scr(U)$), then inside $scr(U)$ they
  have the same number of zeros (counting multiplicities).
] <prop:rouche>

It is not difficult to see that the mappings $f$ and $f + g$ are homotopic on
$partial scr(U)$ if $|f| > |g|$ on $partial scr(U)$ or if for each point
$z in partial scr(U)$ and at least one index $j in {1, ..., n}$ we have
$|f_j (z)| > |g_j (z)|$; in these cases it is sufficient to produce the linear
homotopy $F(t, z) = f + t g$.

#subsection[Representation of a local residue in terms of an integral over the
  boundary] <ss:boundary-representation>
The local residue $res_a omega$ of the form $omega = h d z \/ f$ was defined in
@ss:integral-definition as an $n$-dimensional integral. It turns out that a
local residue can be represented as a $(2n - 1)$-dimensional integral.

#proposition[
  Suppose that a holomorphic mapping $f: overline(scr(U))_a -> CC^n$ is such
  that $overline(scr(U))_a inter f^(-1) (0) = {a}$, and that the boundary
  $partial scr(U)_a$ is piecewise smooth. Then for the local residue of the form
  $omega = omega(f) = h d z \/ f$ we have the representation
  $res_a omega = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) eta_omega$, where
  $
    eta_omega = eta_(omega(f)) = frac((n - 1)!, (2 pi i)^n) h/(|f|^(2n))
    sum_(k = 1)^n (-1)^(k - 1) overline(f)_k overline(d f) [k] and d z.
  $ <eq:eta-form>
] <prop:boundary-integral>

The proof of the proposition will rely on the following fact, which we will need
later.

Let $f: scr(U) -> CC^n$ be a holomorphic mapping of a domain
$scr(U) subset CC^n$ and $epsilon = (epsilon_1, ..., epsilon_n) in RR_>^n$. We
denote by $Pi^epsilon (f)$#notation-mark(
  $Pi^epsilon (f)$,
  sort: "Pi epsilon f",
) the polyhedron ${z in scr(U): |f_j (z)| < epsilon_j, j = 1, ..., n}$, and by
$Gamma^epsilon (f)$#notation-mark(
  $Gamma^epsilon (f)$,
  sort: "Gamma epsilon f",
) its distinguished boundary#footnote[sometimes called its skeleton or
  distinguished set (editor's remark).]
${z in scr(U): |f_j (z)| = epsilon_j, j = 1, ..., n}$.

#lemma[
  If the polyhedron $Pi = Pi^epsilon (f)$ is relatively compact in $scr(U)$ and
  $zeta = (zeta_1, ..., zeta_n) in CC^n$ is a vector such that
  $|zeta_j| < epsilon_j$, $j = 1, ..., n$, then for any
  $delta = (delta_1, ..., delta_n)$, $0 < delta_j < epsilon_j - |zeta_j|$, in
  the domain
  $
    scr(U)_* = scr(U) without {z: product_(j = 1)^n [f_j (z) - zeta_j] = 0}
  $
  we have a homology of cycles: $Gamma^delta (f - zeta) ~ Gamma^epsilon (f)$.
] <lem:homology-cycles>

#proof[
  Consider the following $(n + 1)$-dimensional chain
  $c = c(zeta) = {z in scr(U): |f_j (z) - t zeta_j| = epsilon_j - t|zeta_j|,
    j = 1, ..., n; 0 <= t <= 1}$. It lies in the closure $overline(Pi)$. Indeed,
  if $z^0 in scr(U) without overline(Pi)$, then $|f_k (z^0)| > epsilon_k$ for
  some $k in {1, ..., n}$. Consequently,
  $|f_k (z^0) - t zeta_k| > epsilon_k - t|zeta_k|$, and this implies that
  $z^0 in.not |c|$. Thus, $c$ is a compact chain. Furthermore,
  $|c| subset scr(U)_*$. In fact, suppose that $z in |c|$; then for each $j$
  from 1 to $n$ we have
  $|f_j (z) - zeta_j| >= |f_j (z) - t zeta_j| - (1 - t)|zeta_j|
  = epsilon_j - |zeta_j| > 0$. Finally, for any $delta_j < epsilon_j - |zeta_j|$
  the supports #source(31) $|Gamma^delta (f - zeta)|$ lie in $scr(U)_*$ and
  $Gamma^delta (f - zeta) ~ Gamma^(epsilon - |zeta|) (f - zeta)
  = partial c + Gamma^epsilon (f)$
  (here $|zeta| = (|zeta_1|, ..., |zeta_n|)$), hence we obtain the assertion of
  the lemma.
]

#proof(title: [Proof of the proposition.])[
  We observe that the differential form $eta_omega$ is connected with the
  Martinelli-Bochner kernel in the following way:
  $eta_(omega(f)) = h beta(f) \/ J_f$. We first consider _case_ 1: $a$ is a
  simple zero of the mapping $f$, that is, the Jacobian $J_f (a) != 0$. Then the
  mapping $w = f(z)$ admits a holomorphic inverse $z^(-1) (w)$ and by Cauchy's
  formula we have
  $
    res_a omega
    & = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a^epsilon (f)) h J_f d z \/ J_f dot f \
    & = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma_a^epsilon (w))
    h(z^(-1) (w)) d w \/ J_f (z^(-1) (w)) dot w_1 dots.c w_n \
    & = h(a) \/ J_f (a).
  $
  At the same time, in accordance with the integral representation
  @eq:martinelli-bochner of @ss:geometric-multiplicity[subsection],
  $
    integral_(partial scr(U)_a) eta_(omega(f))
    = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) frac(h(z), J_f (z)) beta(f)
    = integral_(f_\# (partial scr(U)_a))
    frac(h(z^(-1) (w)), J_f (z^(-1) (w))) beta(w) = frac(h(a), J_f (a)).
  $
  We have thus proved our assertion for the case of a simple zero.

  _Case_ 2: $a$ is a multiple zero of the mapping $f$. By
  Proposition~@prop:multiplicity from @ss:geometric-multiplicity[subsection],
  for almost all $zeta in scr(B)_epsilon$ the mapping $w = f(z) - zeta$ has only
  simple zeros $z^((nu)) (zeta)$, $nu = 1, ..., mu$, in $scr(U)_a$. Applying the
  assertion of the lemma to the set $scr(U) = scr(U)_a$ and the cycles
  $Gamma_a = Gamma^epsilon (f)$, $Gamma^delta (f - zeta)$, we have
  $
    res_a omega & = (2 pi i)^(-n)
                  integral_(Gamma^epsilon (f)) (h d z)/f
                  = lim_(zeta -> 0) (2 pi i)^(-n)
                  integral_(Gamma^epsilon (f)) (h d z)/(f - zeta) \
                & = lim_(zeta -> 0) (2 pi i)^(-n)
                  integral_(Gamma^delta (f - zeta)) (h d z)/(f - zeta).
  $
  Hence in accordance with the obvious homology
  $Gamma^delta (f - zeta) ~ sum_(nu = 1)^mu Gamma_(z^((nu)) (zeta))$, where
  $Gamma_(z^((nu)) (zeta)) = {z in scr(U)_(z^((nu)) (zeta)):
    |f_j (z) - zeta_j| = rho_j << delta_j, j = 1, ..., n}$
  (here $scr(U)_(z^((nu)) (zeta))$ are disjoint neighborhoods of the points
  $z^((nu)) (zeta)$), and the assertion proved in case 1 (simple zero) we obtain
  $
    res_a omega & = lim_(zeta -> 0) sum_(nu = 1)^mu (2 pi i)^(-n)
                  integral_(Gamma_(z^((nu)) (zeta))) (h d z)/(f - zeta)
                  = lim_(zeta -> 0) sum_(nu = 1)^mu
                  res_(z^((nu)) (zeta)) ""_(f - zeta) (h) \
                & = lim_(zeta -> 0) sum_(nu = 1)^mu
                  integral_(partial scr(U)_(z^((nu)) (zeta)))
                  eta_(omega(f - zeta))
                  = lim_(zeta -> 0) integral_(partial scr(U)_a)
                  eta_(omega(f - zeta))
                  = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) eta_(omega(f)).
  $
  Here the penultimate equality follows from Stokes's formula, since
  $partial scr(U)_a ~ sum_(nu = 1)^mu partial scr(U)_(z^((nu)) (zeta))$ in the
  domain of regularity of the closed form $eta_(omega(f - zeta))$, and the last
  equality follows from the fact that this form is continuous with respect to
  $zeta$. This proves the proposition.
]

#subsection[The continuity principle] <ss:continuity-principle>
#index-mark(
  "Principle",
  sub: "of continuity",
  target: <ss:continuity-principle>,
)#source(32)Suppose we are given a family of holomorphic mappings
$f_t: overline(scr(U))_a -> CC^n$ that depend continuously on the parameter $t$,
which ranges over some manifold $T$. We assume that for $t = t_0$ the mapping
$f_(t_0)$ has an isolated zero at the point
$a: overline(scr(U))_a inter f_(t_0)^(-1) (0) = {a}$. Then for $t$ close to
$t_0$ the mappings $f_t$ do not have zeros on the boundary $partial scr(U)_a$,
and so they have finitely many zeros ${P_t} = f_t^(-1) (0)$ in $scr(U)_a$. For
the form $omega_t = h_t (z) d z \/ f_t (z)$, where $h_t (z)$ is a function
holomorphic with respect to $z$ and continuous with respect to $t$, at each
point $P_t$ there is defined a local residue $res_(P_t) omega_t$. The next
assertion shows that the local residue is continuous with respect to
"perturbations" of the mapping $f_(t_0)$.

#proposition[
  $
    lim_(t -> t_0) sum_(P_t in f_t^(-1) (0) inter scr(U)_a)
    res_(P_t) omega_t = res_a omega_(t_0).
  $
] <prop:continuity>

#proof[
  The proposition follows from the representation of the local residue by an
  integral over the boundary of the neighborhood (see
  @ss:boundary-representation[subsection]):
  $
    lim_(t -> t_0) sum_({P_t}) res_(P_t) omega_t
    & = lim_(t -> t_0) sum_({P_t}) integral_(partial scr(U)_(P_t))
    eta_(omega_t)
    = lim_(t -> t_0) integral_(partial scr(U)_a) eta_(omega_t) \
    & = integral_(partial scr(U)_a) eta_(omega_(t_0))
    = res_a omega_(t_0).
  $
]

We note that we have such a transparent proof of the proposition thanks to the
representation of the local residue by an integral over the boundary of the
domain. Although the integrand $eta_omega$ of such an integral is not
holomorphic, in contrast to the form $omega$ itself, nevertheless it has a very
simple singularity---isolated zeros of the mapping $f$ and consequently simple
topology of the domain of regularity of $eta_omega$. In practice it is
apparently more convenient to calculate the local residue by using the original
definition as an integral over the distinguished boundary. The transformation
formula that we now give is very useful.

#subsection[Transformation formula] <ss:transformation-formula>
#index-mark(
  "Formula",
  sub: "transformation",
  target: <ss:transformation-formula>,
)Let us explain how a local residue with respect to a mapping changes on
transition to another mapping. Suppose that a mapping $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$ has
an isolated zero at a point $a in CC^n$ and that $g = A f$, where
$A = norm(a_(i j) (z))$ is a matrix with elements $a_(i j)$ holomorphic in a
neighborhood $scr(U)_a$ of the point $a$, that is, $g = (g_1, ..., g_n)$,
$g_j (z) = sum_(k = 1)^n a_(j k) (z) f_k (z)$.

#proposition[
  If holomorphic mappings $f$ and $g = A f$ both have an isolated zero at the
  point $a$, then for any $h in scr(O)_a$
  $
    res_a ""_f (h) = res_a ""_g (h dot det A).
  $ <eq:transformation>
] <prop:transformation>

#proof[
  The proof is divided into a number of stages.

  #source(33)_Stage_ 1: the Jacobian $J_f (a) != 0$, $det A(a) != 0$. Obviously
  $J_g (a) = det A(a) dot J_f (a)$, so direct calculation by means of Cauchy's
  formula gives
  $
    res_a ""_f (h)
    & = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma^epsilon (f)) frac(h J_f, J_f f) d z
    = frac(h(a), J_f (a)); \
    res_a ""_g (h dot det A)
    & = frac(h(a) det A(a), J_g (a)) = frac(h(a), J_f (a)).
  $
  #new-par
  _Stage_ 2: $J_f (a) = 0$, $det A(a) != 0$. By Proposition~@prop:multiplicity
  of @ss:geometric-multiplicity[subsection] there is a sequence
  ${zeta^((k))} subset CC^n$, $zeta^((k)) -> 0$, such that the mappings
  $w = f(z) - zeta^((k))$ have only simple zeros
  ${P_nu^((k)), nu = 1, ..., mu}$. Then on the basis of Stage 1 and the
  principle of continuity we have
  $
    res_a ""_f (h) & = lim_(k -> infinity) sum_(nu = 1)^mu
                     res_(P_nu^((k)))
                     ""_((f - zeta^((k)))) (h) \
                   & = lim_(k -> infinity) sum_(nu = 1)^mu
                     res_(P_nu^((k)))
                     ""_(A(f - zeta^((k)))) (h det A)
                     = res_a ""_(A f) (h det A).
  $
  #new-par
  _Stage_ 3: $det A(a) = 0$. It is easy to see that the set of all nonsingular
  matrices is everywhere dense in the set of all matrices. Therefore there is a
  family of nonsingular matrices ${A_t}$, $t in [0, 1]$, such that $A_t -> A$ as
  $t -> 0$. Let us apply the result of Stage 2 to the mappings $f$ and
  $g_t = A_t f$. We must take into account that the set of zeros ${P_t}$ of the
  mapping $g_t$ may contain, apart from the point $a$, other points that
  converge to $a$ as $t -> 0$. However, the next lemma shows that the residues
  $res_(P_t) ""_(g_t) (h det A_t)$, $P_t != a$, at these points are equal to
  zero.
]

#lemma[
  Suppose that in a domain $G subset CC^n$ we are given a mapping $f: G -> CC^n$
  and that $g = A f$ for some functional matrix $A = norm(a_(i j) (z))$,
  $a_(i j) in scr(O)(G)$. If for some point $P in G$ we have $g(P) = 0$,
  $f(P) != 0$, then $det A in I_P (g)$, and consequently (see the proposition of
  @ss:integral-definition[subsection]) $res_P ""_g (h det A) = 0$ for any
  $h in scr(O)_P$.
] <lem:det-in-ideal>

#proof[
  For definiteness we assume that the component $f_1$ of the mapping
  $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$ is nonzero at the point $P$. Then in a neighborhood of
  this point we can write
  $a_(k 1) = g_k \/ f_1 - sum_(q = 2)^n a_(k q) f_q \/ f_1$. Let $A_(k j)$ be
  the algebraic complements of the element $a_(k j)$. By well-known properties,
  $
    det A & = sum_(k = 1)^n a_(k 1) A_(k 1)
            = sum_(k = 1)^n [g_k \/ f_1 - sum_(q = 2)^n a_(k q) f_q \/ f_1]
            A_(k 1) \
          & = sum_(k = 1)^n g_k [A_(k 1) \/ f_1] in I_P (g).
  $
  #source(34)The assertion of Stage 3 now follows from the continuity principle,
  the lemma, and the assertion of Stage 2:
  $
    res_a ""_g (h det A)
    & = lim_(t -> 0) sum_({P_t}) res_(P_t) ""_(g_t) (h det A_t) \
    & = lim_(t -> 0) res_a ""_(g_t) (h det A_t)
    = lim_(t -> 0) res_a ""_f (h) \
    & = res_a ""_f (h).
  $
  This completely proves the proposition.
]

The transformation formula @eq:transformation gives a way of calculating the
local residue. For example, putting in the monomial $(z_j - a_j)^(k_j + 1)$ for
$g_j$ (according to a local variant of Hilbert's theorem on zeros, which will be
proved in @ss:local-nullstellensatz, any such monomial of sufficiently high
degree lies in the ideal $I_a (f)$), we arrive at the formula
$
  res_a ""_f (h) = frac(1, k!) frac(partial^(|k|), partial z^k)
  [h det A]|_(z = a).
$ <eq:residue-derivatives>
From this formula it is obvious that the local residue is expressed in terms of
finitely many derivatives of $h$ at $a$.

Sometimes in practical calculations we need to find the local residue with
respect to a mapping of the form $(f_1^(k_1), ..., f_n^(k_n))$, where the $f_j$
are fixed functions, and $k_j$ are varying natural numbers. In this case we can
apply the transformation formula as follows. Starting from the representations
(not depending on $k = (k_1, ..., k_n)$)
$g_j = a_(j 1) f_1 + dots.c + a_(j n) f_n$, $j = 1, ..., n$, it is not difficult
to calculate for each $k$ an expansion of the form
$
  g_j^(|k|) = B_(j 1)^((k)) f_1^(k_1) + dots.c + B_(j n)^((k)) f_n^(k_n),
  wide j = 1, ..., n,
$
where $|k| = k_1 + dots.c + k_n$, $B_(j q)^((k))$ are polynomials in the
elements of the matrix $A = norm(a_(i j))$ and the vector $f = (f_1, ..., f_n)$,
and the degree of $B_(j q)^((k))$ with respect to $f$ is equal to $|k| - k_q$.
Then by the transformation formula @eq:transformation
$
  integral_(Gamma^epsilon (f)) frac(h d z, f_1^(k_1) dots.c f_n^(k_n))
  = integral_(Gamma^delta (g))
  frac(h det norm(B_(j q)^((k))) d z, g_1^(|k|) dots.c g_n^(|k|)).
$ <eq:power-transformation>
#new-par
We cannot call formula @eq:power-transformation complete, because the procedure
for calculating the determinant $det norm(B_(j q)^((k)))$, which depends on $k$,
is immense; in addition, on the left-hand side the total degree of the
denominator is equal to $|k|$, and on the right-hand side it is considerably
higher, which may complicate the calculation. It turns out that the right-hand
side in @eq:power-transformation can be represented as the sum of the analogous
integrals in which the total degree of the denominator is equal to $|k|$, and
the numerators are written explicitly. The existence of such a representation is
immediately obvious when $det A(a) != 0$: in this case $f = A^(-1) g$, from
which it follows that $B_(j q)^((k))$ consists of terms of the #source(35) form
$phi g^alpha$, $phi in scr(O)_a$, $|alpha| = |k| - k_q$; correspondingly
$det norm(B_(j q)^((k)))$ is composed of terms of the same form, where
$|alpha| = n|k| - |k|$. In order to write down the exact formula, it is more
convenient to use another form of the multi-index notation. For any multi-index
$I = {i_1, ..., i_m}$, where $1 <= i_1, ..., i_m <= n$, we put
$f_I = f_(i_1) dots.c f_(i_m)$. We denote by $I!$ the product
$k_1 ! dots.c k_n !$, where $k_1$ is the number of ones in $I$, $k_2$ is the
number of twos, and so on.

#proposition[
  If the holomorphic mappings $f$ and $g = A f$ have an isolated zero at the
  point $a$, then for any multi-index $I$ of length $m$#ed-note[Formula
    @eq:kytmanov is missing in the edition of 1992; it is restored from the
    equality to which its proof is reduced below, by Cauchy's formula.]
  $
    I! integral_(Gamma^epsilon (f)) frac(h d z, f_I f_1 dots.c f_n)
    = sum_J J! integral_(Gamma^delta (g))
    frac(h det A dot a_(j_1 i_1) dots.c a_(j_m i_m) d z, g_J g_1 dots.c g_n),
  $ <eq:kytmanov>
  where $a_(j k)$ are elements of the matrix $A$, and the summation on the right
  is carried out over all multi-indices $J = (j_1, ..., j_m)$ of length $m$.
] <prop:kytmanov>

We can prove formula @eq:kytmanov by the scheme of the proof of
Proposition~@prop:transformation, where the Stages 2 and 3 are repeated
literally. Stage 1, which corresponds to the case $J_f (a) != 0$,
$det A(a) != 0$, consists in reducing the problem, by changes of variables
$w = f(z)$ on the left-hand side and $w = g(z)$ on the right-hand side on the
basis of Cauchy's formula, to the proof of the equality
$
  & frac(partial^m, partial w_I)
    lr([frac(h, J_f) (f^(-1) (w))] |)_(w = 0) \
  & quad = sum_J frac(partial^m, partial w_J)
    lr(
      [frac(h det A dot a_(j_1 i_1) dots.c a_(j_m i_m), J_g)
        (g^(-1) (w))] |
    )_(w = 0),
$
where $partial^m \/ partial w_I
= partial^m \/ partial w_(i_1) dots.c partial w_(i_m)$. We omit the proof of
this equality.

#subsection[Local duality theorem] <ss:local-duality>
Clearly, the local residue $res_a ""_f (h)$ is linear in $h in scr(O)_a$, and
according to @ss:integral-definition[subsection] it is equal to zero for germs
$h$ of the ideal $I_a (f)$. Consequently, the linear functional (linear form)
$res_a ""_f: scr(O)_a \/ I_a (f) -> CC$ is well defined. Since, by the local
variant of Hilbert's theorem on roots, any monomial $(z - a)^alpha$ of
sufficiently high degree $|alpha|$ lies in the ideal $I_a (f)$, the quotient
$Q_(f, a) = scr(O)_a \/ I_a (f)$#notation-mark(
  $Q_(f, a)$,
  sort: "Q f a",
) is a finite-dimensional vector space over the field $CC$. Since it also has
the structure of a ring, $Q_(f, a)$ is an algebra, which is called the
#index-mark("Algebra, local")_local algebra of the mapping $f$ at the point
$a$_. Taking account of what we have said, we have a bilinear form
$
  B(dot, dot): Q_(f, a) times Q_(f, a) -> CC
$
defined by the formula $B(hat(h), hat(g)) = res_a ""_f (h g)$
($hat(h)$#notation-mark($hat(h)$, sort: "h"), $hat(g)$ denote the classes of
germs in $Q_(f, a)$ generated by the representations $h, g in scr(O)_a$).

#theorem[
  The bilinear form $B(hat(h), hat(g))$ is nondegenerate, that is, if
  $res_a ""_f (h g) = 0$ for all $g in scr(O)_a$, then $h in I_a (f)$.
] <th:local-duality>

From linear algebra it is known that any nondegenerate bilinear form on a vector
space $E$ induces an isomorphism between $E$ and its dual space $E^*$. In our
case this implies that for any linear (functional) $l$ on $Q_(f, a)$ #source(36)
there is an element $h in scr(O)_a$ such that $l(hat(g)) = res_a ""_f (h g)$,
$hat(g) in Q_(f, a)$. For the reason we have stated, the theorem on
nondegeneracy of the bilinear form $B(hat(h), hat(g)) = res_a ""_f (h g)$ is
called the #index-mark(
  "Theorem",
  sub: "of duality local",
)_local duality theorem._

#proof[
  The proof follows immediately from Weil's integral representation, which we
  derive from the transformation formula of a local residue in
  @ss:weil-representation. Indeed, let $h$ be a holomorphic function in some
  neighborhood $scr(U)$ of the point $a$ and suppose that $res_a ""_f (h g) = 0$
  for all $g in scr(O)_a$. About the neighborhood $scr(U)$ we assume that
  $f|_(scr(U))$ vanishes only at the point $a$. We represent $h$ by Weil's
  formula as
  $
    h(z) = (2 pi i)^(-n) integral_Gamma
    h(zeta) frac(H(zeta, z) d zeta, f(zeta) - f(z)), wide z in Pi,
  $
  where $Pi$ is the polyhedron defined by the conditions $|f_j (z)| < epsilon_j$
  and $Pi relcomp scr(U)$, and $Gamma$ is the framework of this polyhedron.
  Thus, when $z in Pi$, $zeta in Gamma$ we have $|f_j (z)| < |f_j (zeta)|$ for
  all $j$ from 1 to $n$, so the integrand decomposes into a series of multiple
  geometric progression
  $
    h dot H [f(zeta) - f(z)]^(-1)
    = h dot H sum_(abs(alpha) >= 0) frac(f^alpha (z), f^(alpha + I) (zeta)),
  $
  which converges uniformly on compact sets of $Gamma times Pi$. As a result of
  term-by-term integration we obtain
  $
    h(z) = res_a ""_f (h(zeta) dot H(zeta, z))
    + sum_(abs(alpha) >= 1) [(2 pi i)^(-n) integral_Gamma
      frac(h(zeta) H(zeta, z) d zeta, f^(alpha + I) (zeta))] f^alpha (z).
  $
  It now remains to observe that by hypothesis the first term is zero (we
  observe that $H$ is a holomorphic function), and the second belongs to
  $I_a (f)$.
]

From formula @eq:residue-derivatives of @ss:transformation-formula[subsection]
it follows that the local residue, as a functional on $scr(O)_a$, has the form
$
  res_a ""_f (h) = sum_(abs(alpha) <= m) c_alpha D^alpha h(a),
$
where $m$ depends only on the mapping $f$ (here
$D^alpha = partial^(|alpha|) \/ partial z^alpha$). Thus, $res_a ""_f$ is an
analytic functional of finite type, that is, it is equal to a finite linear
combination of derivatives of the delta-function at the point $a$. There arises
the question: can any analytic functional of finite type
$scr(L) = sum_(abs(alpha) <= m) c_alpha D^alpha delta(z - a)$ be realized as a
local residue with respect to some mapping at $a$? The answer is negative, and
the local duality theorem helps to verify it. In fact, let us associate with a
given functional $scr(L)$ the ideal $I_(scr(L)) subset scr(O)_a$ consisting of
elements $h in scr(O)_a$ such that $scr(L) (h dot g) = 0$ for all
$g in scr(O)_a$. From the local duality theorem it follows that if
$scr(L) = res_a ""_f$ for some mapping $f$, then $I_(scr(L)) = I_a (f)$. Since
for mappings $f$ in $CC^n$ the ideal $I_a (f)$ has $n$ generators (it is
$n$-generated), we conclude that $scr(L)$ cannot be realized by a local residue
#source(37) if the ideal $I_(scr(L))$ associated with it is not $n$-generated.
An example of such a functional is
$scr(L) = sum_(abs(alpha) = 2) D^alpha delta(z)$ when $n >= 3$. Let us prove
this. Since
$
  scr(L) (h g) = sum_(1 <= i <= j <= n) lr(
    (
      h frac(partial^2 g, partial z_i partial z_j)
      + frac(partial h, partial z_i) frac(partial g, partial z_j)
      + frac(partial h, partial z_j) frac(partial g, partial z_i)
      + g frac(partial^2 h, partial z_i partial z_j)
    ) |
  )_(z = 0),
$
the condition $scr(L) (h dot g) = 0$ for all $g in scr(O)_0$ implies that
$h(0) = 0$, and the vector
$c = ((partial h \/ partial z_1)(0), ..., (partial h \/ partial z_n)(0))$
satisfies a system of linear equations $A c = 0$ with matrix $A$ that has twos
on the diagonal and ones everywhere else. Such a matrix is nonsingular, so
$(partial h \/ partial z_j)(0) = 0$, $j = 1, ..., n$. Second derivatives of $h$
at zero are connected by the relation
$sum_(i <= j) partial^2 h \/ partial z_i partial z_j = 0$. As a result we see
that the elements $h$ of $I_(scr(L))$ have the form
$sum_(abs(alpha) >= 2) c_alpha z^alpha$, where the second order coefficients are
connected by one linear equation. The number of second order coefficients is
equal to $(n + 1) n \/ 2$, so the ideal $I_(scr(L))$ must have at least
$(n + 1) n \/ 2 - 1$ generators, that is, more than $n$ generators if $n >= 3$.

In the case $n = 2$, however, every analytic functional of finite type is
realized by a local residue.#ed-note[The 1992 edition states the opposite: that
  for $n = 2$ there are functionals of finite type that are not local residues,
  for example $sum_(abs(alpha) = 3) D^alpha delta(z)$. The algebra
  $scr(O)_0 \/ I_(scr(L))$ is Gorenstein, and for $n = 2$ such an ideal is
  generated by two elements (Serre); see Eisenbud @bib:Eisenbud1995,
  Theorem~21.6 and Corollary~21.20.] The reader can verify that, for example,
the functional $scr(L) = sum_(abs(alpha) = 3) D^alpha delta(z)$ is equal to
$res_0 ""_g$ for $g = ((z_1^2 - 4 z_1 z_2 + z_2^2) \/ 4, z_1 z_2 (z_1 - z_2))$.
